   "id": "spcpt_rrxbKqWZ3TqMn52g",
   "name": "Radian Measure",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Radian Measure"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are radians?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Radians** are an alternative to degrees for measuring angles\n- 1 radian is **defined** as the angle in a circle **sector** of radius 1 and arc length 1\n- Radians are often given in terms of $π$\n\n  - $2π$ radians = 360°\n  - $π$ radians = 180°\n- The **symbol** for radians is <sup>c</sup> but it is more usual to see **rad**\n\n  - When $π$ is involved, **no symbol** for radians is needed\n  \n    - With $π$ it is obvious it is in radians\n  - You should **never** omit the symbol for degrees\n- In the exam a question will usually tell you **which angle measure** to use\n\n  - But radians **must** be used if calculus with the trigonometric functions is involved"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/03/5-4-1-radian-measure-notes-diagram-1.png",
   "file": "assets/7b5234c20288-5-4-1-radian-measure-notes-diagram-1.png",
   "alt": "1 radian shown on a unit circle",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I convert between radians and degrees?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Use $π$* *radians<sup> </sup>= 180° to **convert** between radians and degrees\n\n  - To convert **from radians to degrees** multiply by $\\frac{180}{π}$\n  - To convert **from degrees to radians** multiply by $\\frac{π}{180}$\n- Some of the **common equivalents** are:\n\n  - $2π\\mathrm{rad}=360^{\\circ}$\n  - $π\\mathrm{rad}=180^{\\circ}$\n  - $\\frac{π}{2}\\mathrm{rad}=90^{\\circ}$\n  - $\\frac{π}{3}\\mathrm{rad}=60^{\\circ}$\n  - $\\frac{π}{4}\\mathrm{rad}=45^{\\circ}$\n  - $\\frac{π}{6}\\mathrm{rad}=30^{\\circ}$\n- It is useful to **remember** these and use them to work out other conversions\n- Your **calculator** will be able to work with both radians and degrees"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/03/5-4-1-radian-measure-notes-diagram-2.png",
   "file": "assets/bb6a9c0e8fdb-5-4-1-radian-measure-notes-diagram-2.png",
   "alt": "using multiples or fractions of pi when converting between radians and degrees",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- If the question specifies a unit of angle measure (radians or degrees) be sure to use it!\n- Make sure that your calculator is in the correct mode for the angle measure you are working with"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "(a) Convert 43.8° to radians, giving your answer correct to 3 significant figures.\n\n> *Multiply by  *$\\frac{π}{180}$\n\n$43.8\\times\\frac{π}{180}=\\frac{73π}{300}=0.764454...$\n\n> *Note that *$\\frac{73π}{300}$* is the exact value answer*\n\n**Final answer:** **0.764 rad (3 s.f.)**\n\n(b) Convert $\\frac{5π}{4}$ radians to degrees.\n\n> *If you remember that *$\\frac{π}{4}$* radians is 45°, then you just need to multiply that by 5 to get the answer.*\n\n> *Otherwise, multiply by  *$\\frac{180}{π}$\n\n$\\frac{5π}{4}\\times\\frac{180}{π}=\\frac{5}{4}\\times180=5\\times45=225$\n\n**Final answer:** **225°**"
  }
 ]
}